# L §4.3 rule 2: "if it reads nothing law-dated, it needs no law date: an input whose rows all govern `always` ... is
# read without one, by a def as by a move".  m2 = n + 1 with n = 2 governs always: 3, with no at and no law.  The move
# pay reads amt and pay_day (governs always) with no at: its leg is amt, 10.00 PLN, on 2026-02-01 (konto from 0 at the opening:
# 10.00 PLN at the end of the year; L §5.14 legs, E §4.2).  A later version giving n a dated row makes m2's reader
# state at: see L4_3_timeless_dated.
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "a source"
unit PLN: currency minor 0.01
place konto: accounts PLN lives 2020-01-01..
place world: outside lives 1900-01-01..

input n: int
input amt: money PLN
input pay_day: date

def m2: int = n + 1

move pay: transfers
  on pay_day
  leg cash from world to konto amount amt

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  n = 2
  amt = 10.00 PLN
  pay_day = 2026-02-01
  opening[konto] = 0 PLN at 2026-01-01 start

ask q1: m2
ask q2: bal(konto, 2026-12-31)
=== expect
q1: 3
  cut 2026-12-31
q2: 10.00 PLN
...
